 "perfora": {
  "c0t": "Tool and interface",
  "c0x": "Perfora started as a way to fold the patterns I kept drawing by hand into a single Rhino tool. Pick a pattern, drag the controllers on the canvas, and apply the result to the model with one click.",
  "i0t": "Launch film",
  "i0d": "A vertical film I made myself, building a graded perforated panel from parameters alone, without wiring up any nodes.",
  "i1t": "Perfora Studio inside Rhino",
  "i1d": "Pattern cards on the left, a canvas with a point controller in the middle, layout and hole settings on the right, with live hole count and open area.",
  "c1t": "Pattern library",
  "c1x": "Facet, lattice and line patterns all share one settings system. The size or depth of each pattern can be driven by an axis, a point or a curve.",
  "i2t": "Facet patterns",
  "i2d": "Eight facet types such as triangular pyramid, diamond knurl and tumbling blocks, on flat plates, a round panel and a cylinder.",
  "i3t": "Parametric line patterns",
  "i3d": "Line previews of eighteen patterns; each name states whether an axis, point or curve drives its size or depth.",
  "c2t": "On surfaces and in production",
  "c2x": "Patterns wrap onto curved surfaces as well as flat panels, and can be split into parts ready to make. This group shows how wrapping and slicing hold up on real geometry.",
  "i4t": "Seam fix",
  "i4d": "Three patterns wrapped on cylinders, before and after version 1.2.5; the break along the pattern's seam line is gone.",
  "i5t": "Slicing",
  "i5d": "A wave wall panel, a lounger and a node form split into cuttable slices, with parts laid out flat and numbered."
 },
